  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A long horizontal rod has a bead which can slide along its length, and initially it is&nbsp;at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math> from the&nbsp;end <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> of the rod. The rod is set in angular motion about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> with constant angular acceleration <math> <mrow> <mi>α</mi> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;in a gravity free space. If the coefficient of friction between the rod and the bead is <math> <mrow> <mi>μ</mi> </mrow> </math>, then the time after which the bead starts slipping is</p>\r",
    "image": null
  },
